// ScaleTestRunner — climb the generated ladder, measure its MEMORY, print it, and record the trend.
//
// IT RENDERS NO VERDICT. There is nothing here to pass.
//
// `spec-test` proves the compiler is CORRECT and gates on it. This is an INSTRUMENT: it compiles a
// ladder of generated programs — six rungs, each double the last — measures ALLOCATIONS, FREES and
// BYTES per phase per rung, prints them, and appends a datapoint to `docs/optimization-log.md`. The
// question it answers is *"what has this compiler's memory traffic actually done, change by change"*,
// not *"may I merge"*.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// MEMORY ONLY, AND NO CURVES. Both deletions, and why.
// ===========================================================================================
//
// IT DOES NOT COLLECT TIME. Wall time is machine-dependent, so a dated table of it compares a loaded
// box in July against an idle one in August and concludes nothing. Allocations and bytes are
// bit-for-bit reproducible: the same compiler on the same corpus produces the same digits whatever
// else the machine is doing, so a number that MOVED, moved for a reason. That is the only column in
// which a trend means anything, so it is the only column there is. (The COMPILER still times itself,
// and should: `--metrics=<path>` and `--log=compiler:debug` give per-phase milliseconds for one
// compile, which is where a clock is worth reading and where nothing is being trended against another
// machine on another day. This runner reads those metrics files and ignores their time column.)
//
// AND IT FITS NOTHING. The ladder DOUBLES, so the ratio between two consecutive rungs IS the growth,
// read straight off the raw numbers — x2.00 is linear, x4.00 is quadratic. A fitted exponent could
// say nothing that the doubling had not already said, and it dragged a residual behind it, and the
// residual dragged a NOISY verdict, and the NOISY verdict got an exemption written for it so that it
// would stop complaining. That is optimizing the gauge instead of the engine, and it is only ever
// tempting when there is a gauge that can be wrong. A ratio is a division: there is no light to turn
// green, so a surprising number is a READING TO EXPLAIN. See ScaleRatio.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// WHAT AN EXIT CODE STILL MEANS
// ===========================================================================================
//
// Exit 0 on any successful run, whatever the numbers say. Non-zero ONLY when the run itself broke —
// it could not measure, or could not deliver what it measured:
//
//   - a rung would not compile,
//   - a metrics file was missing or malformed,
//   - the generated corpus was DEGENERATE — it folded away, so nothing was measured at all
//     (ScaleCorpus.assertCorpusCompiled),
//   - the trend log could not be read, or a `--note` could not honestly be recorded in it,
//   - a `--result-json` document was asked for and could not be written. The caller that asks for
//     that file is a MACHINE, and a run that answers it with an exit code of 0 and no file has told
//     it a successful-looking lie.
//
// A broken MEASUREMENT is a broken run. A SURPRISING NUMBER is not. The report says which happened,
// loudly, and never confuses the two.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// A SUBPROCESS PER RUNG, ONE REPETITION
// ===========================================================================================
//
// shv2 has no on-disk cache — `Compiler.loadProject` reads every source fresh in every process — so
// a new process is COLD BY CONSTRUCTION and there is nothing to bust. Spawning also means a crash
// at the top rung kills that rung and not the run, which is not hypothetical: the 19,200-block
// shape at rung 5 is exactly where the historical SIGSEGV lived, and a runner that died with it
// would report nothing at all about the five rungs that had already climbed.
//
// ONE REPETITION, and now it is not even a compromise. Everything measured here is bit-for-bit
// reproducible, so a second repetition returns the same digits — there is nothing to average out and
// no reason to pay for it. (This was a real compromise while the suite still timed itself: wall time
// at n=1 is noisy. It stopped being one when the clock was removed.)

// A rung's metrics TSV and its executable, in the corpus ROOT — NEVER inside the rung's own source
// directory.
//
// That is a correctness requirement, not a filing preference. `build <dir>` LISTS the directory it
// is given, and every entry costs the compiler a `FilePath` and a `String`: a rung whose own output
// sits beside its sources measures a different program than the same rung on a fresh checkout. It
// used to, by exactly 26 allocations and 945 bytes per rung, and the "reproducible" memory numbers
// were reproducible only on a machine that had run the suite before. See ScaleCorpus's header, and
// `pruneRungDir`, which enforces the other half of the invariant.
let RungMetricsSuffix = ".metrics.tsv"
let RungOutputSuffix = ".out"

// Shared with ScalePerType, which spawns the same `build` command against a --mm-trace compiler.
export let BuildSubcommandName = "build"
export let OutputFlagName = "-o"

// The compiler must be given a RELATIVE rung path, and this is load-bearing rather than tidy.
//
// The byte counters count every String the compiler allocates, and a source file's PATH is one of
// them. An absolute path is as long as wherever the repo happens to live, so the same rung compiled
// from `C:/dev/maxon` and from `/home/e/src/maxon-checkout` would allocate a different number of
// BYTES — and the byte column of the trend log, which is a fact about the compiler and not about the
// checkout, would read differently for every developer. A relative path is the same string on every
// machine. The subprocess inherits this process's working directory, which is the repo root (see
// Main.resolveSpecDir, which relies on the same).
export let CorpusRelativeRoot = ".scale-tmp"

// Everything in the scale suite that can go wrong at RUN time: a rung that would not compile, a
// metrics file that never appeared, a trend log that could not be read.
//
// A union carrying its own message, because Maxon errors are enums/unions and a message is the only
// payload any of these needs — every one of them is reported to a human and none is recovered from
// programmatically. `scaleErrorText` is the single place it is unwrapped.
export union ScaleError implements Error
	reason(message String)
end 'ScaleError'

export function scaleErrorText(error ScaleError) returns String
	return match error 'e'
		reason(message) gives message
	end 'e'
end 'scaleErrorText'

// One phase's numbers, as parsed back out of a rung's metrics TSV.
//
// NO `nanos` FIELD. The TSV still carries a nanoseconds column — the compiler times itself, and
// should — and this suite reads straight past it. Carrying it here would mean carrying it into the
// report, the JSON and the log, and a machine-dependent number in a trend table is a number that
// says more about what else the box was doing than about the compiler. See this file's header.
export type PhaseMetric
	export let allocs as AllocCount
	export let frees as AllocCount
	export let bytes as ByteCount

	export static function create(allocs AllocCount, frees AllocCount, bytes ByteCount) returns PhaseMetric
		return Self{allocs: allocs, frees: frees, bytes: bytes}
	end 'create'

	export static function zero() returns PhaseMetric
		return Self{allocs: 0, frees: 0, bytes: 0}
	end 'zero'
end 'PhaseMetric'

export typealias PhaseMetricArray = Array with PhaseMetric

// Everything one rung reported. `phases` and `regAlloc` are dense, indexed by their enum's ordinal,
// so a phase that failed to appear in the TSV is a parse ERROR rather than a silent zero — see
// `parseRungMetrics`.
export type RungResult
	export let rung as RungIndex
	export let sourceBytes as SourceByteCount
	export let codeBytes as CodeByteCount
	export let total as PhaseMetric
	export let phases as PhaseMetricArray
	export let regAlloc as PhaseMetricArray

	export static function create(rung RungIndex, sourceBytes SourceByteCount, codeBytes CodeByteCount, total PhaseMetric, phases PhaseMetricArray, regAlloc PhaseMetricArray) returns RungResult
		return Self{rung: rung, sourceBytes: sourceBytes, codeBytes: codeBytes, total: total, phases: phases, regAlloc: regAlloc}
	end 'create'

	export function phaseAt(phase CompilePhase) returns PhaseMetric
		return try self.phases.get(phase.ordinal) otherwise panic("RungResult.phaseAt: phases.get OOB for '{phase.name}'")
	end 'phaseAt'

	export function regAllocAt(sub RegAllocPhase) returns PhaseMetric
		return try self.regAlloc.get(sub.ordinal) otherwise panic("RungResult.regAllocAt: regAlloc.get OOB for '{sub.name}'")
	end 'regAllocAt'
end 'RungResult'

export typealias RungResultArray = Array with RungResult

// ===========================================================================================
// The phases this instrument reports, enumerated ONCE
// ===========================================================================================

// WHAT one row of the per-phase table is measured from: a compile phase, or one of the register
// allocator's sub-phases.
//
// The sub-phases are a BREAKDOWN of `phase:regalloc` and not extra phases beside it — adding them to
// the same list would double-count every allocation the allocator makes — so they are enumerated
// after the phases and printed under them.
//
// ONE ENUMERATION, walked by the human table and by `--result-json` alike. Two hand-maintained walks
// of the same enums is how a phase gets added to `CompilePhase`, timed, metered, and then silently
// omitted from one output but not the other; and a name built at two sites is a name that can differ
// at two sites. The list, and the naming, live here.
export union PhaseSource
	compilePhase(phase CompilePhase)
	regAllocPhase(sub RegAllocPhase)
end 'PhaseSource'

export typealias PhaseSourceArray = Array with PhaseSource

let PhaseNamePrefix = "phase:"
let RegAllocNamePrefix = "regalloc:"

export function scalePhaseSources() returns PhaseSourceArray
	var sources = PhaseSourceArray.create()

	for phase in CompilePhase.allCases 'eachPhase'
		sources.push(PhaseSource.compilePhase(phase))
	end 'eachPhase'

	for sub in RegAllocPhase.allCases 'eachSub'
		sources.push(PhaseSource.regAllocPhase(sub))
	end 'eachSub'

	return sources
end 'scalePhaseSources'

export function phaseSourceName(source PhaseSource) returns String
	return match source 'src'
		compilePhase(phase) gives "{PhaseNamePrefix}{phase.name}"
		regAllocPhase(sub) gives "{RegAllocNamePrefix}{sub.name}"
	end 'src'
end 'phaseSourceName'

export function phaseMetricOf(source PhaseSource, result RungResult) returns PhaseMetric
	return match source 'src'
		compilePhase(phase) gives result.phaseAt(phase)
		regAllocPhase(sub) gives result.regAllocAt(sub)
	end 'src'
end 'phaseMetricOf'

// ===========================================================================================
// Entry point
// ===========================================================================================

export function runScaleTest(args MaxonArgs) returns ExitCode
	// `--update-required` is GONE from this command, and saying so is worth four lines. It rewrote
	// committed memory goldens; there are no goldens. It is still a spec-test flag, and the driver
	// parses the command line before it knows which command is running — so without this it would be
	// accepted here and silently do nothing, on a command line every document in this tree used to
	// tell people to type.
	if args.updateRequired 'noGoldens'
		printError("scale-test: --update-required is gone. It rewrote the committed memory goldens, and there are none:\n")
		printError("            this suite renders no verdict and has nothing to accept.\n")
		printError("            To RECORD a run in {OptimizationLogRelativePath}, use --note=\"<what changed and why>\".\n")
		return 1
	end 'noGoldens'

	// The trend is read FIRST, before a single rung is compiled. It is what this run's numbers are
	// reported against, and if a `--note` cannot honestly be recorded in it, saying so after thirty
	// seconds of compiling would be a rude way to report a typo.
	let trend = try loadScaleTrend() otherwise (error) 'noTrend'
		return brokenRun("the trend log could not be read", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'noTrend'

	if not args.note.isEmpty() 'wantsToRecord'
		try assertRecordable(trend, note: args.note, rungs: args.rungs) otherwise (error) 'unrecordable'
			return brokenRun("this run could not be recorded", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
		end 'unrecordable'
	end 'wantsToRecord'

	let compilerExe = try Process.executablePath() otherwise 'noExe'
		return brokenRun("the running compiler's own path could not be determined", detail: "Process.executablePath() failed")
	end 'noExe'

	let corpusRoot = try corpusRootPath(args) otherwise 'badCorpus'
		return brokenRun("the corpus root is not a valid path", detail: "--emit-corpus={args.emitCorpus}")
	end 'badCorpus'

	print("scale-test: generating {args.rungs} rung(s) under {corpusRoot.toString()}\n")
	let ladder = try generateCorpus(corpusRoot, rungs: args.rungs) otherwise (error) 'genErr'
		return brokenRun("the corpus could not be generated", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'genErr'

	// SAY WHAT THIS CORPUS COVERS AND WHAT IT CANNOT, on every run and before any number. An instrument
	// that cannot state its blind spots produces confident zeros — see `printCorpusManifest`.
	printCorpusManifest()

	if not args.emitCorpus.isEmpty() 'corpusOnly'
		print("scale-test: corpus written to {corpusRoot.toString()} (--emit-corpus: not compiling)\n")
		return 0
	end 'corpusOnly'

	printRungHeader()
	var results = RungResultArray.create()
	for rung in ladder 'eachRung'
		let result = try compileRung(compilerExe, root: corpusRoot, rung: rung) otherwise (error) 'rungFailed'
			// A rung that would not compile is a broken run, not a slow one: every later rung is a
			// superset of this one's shapes, so there is nothing left to measure and nothing the rungs
			// below it can be compared against.
			return brokenRun("rung {rung.index} FAILED to compile", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
		end 'rungFailed'
		printRungLine(result, results: results)
		results.push(result)
	end 'eachRung'

	// Check the instrument before the subject. A corpus that folded away measured NOTHING, and every
	// number below it would be a number about nothing — a beautifully proportional set of them.
	try assertCorpusCompiled(results) otherwise (error) 'degenerate'
		return brokenRun("the generated corpus was DEGENERATE, so this run measured nothing", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'degenerate'

	printPhaseTable(results, ofBytes: false)
	printPhaseTable(results, ofBytes: true)
	printMemoryTrend(results, trend: trend)
	printCorpusGrowth(results)

	if not args.resultJson.isEmpty() 'json'
		try writeResultJson(args.resultJson, results: results, trend: trend) otherwise (error) 'jsonFailed'
			return brokenRun("the result document could not be written", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
		end 'jsonFailed'
	end 'json'

	if not args.note.isEmpty() 'record'
		let recorded = recordRun(trend, results: results, note: args.note)
		if recorded != 0 'recordFailed'
			return recorded
		end 'recordFailed'
	end 'record'

	if args.perType 'perTypePass'
		runPerTypePass(results)
	end 'perTypePass'

	return 0
end 'runScaleTest'

// The ONE way this instrument fails: the RUN broke — it could not measure, or could not deliver what
// it measured. It says so in those words, so that a reader can never mistake it for a judgement about
// the numbers; there is no judgement about the numbers.
//
// Each caller names what broke, and the causes are not interchangeable: a degenerate corpus measured
// NOTHING, while a result document that could not be written measured everything and could not hand
// it over. This line therefore says "the run", which is true of both, rather than "nothing was
// measured", which was written for the first and is a lie about the second.
function brokenRun(what String, detail String) returns ExitCode
	printError("\nscale-test: BROKEN RUN — {what}.\n")
	printError("  {detail}\n")
	printError("  The RUN failed. This is not a verdict about the compiler's numbers. Fix it and re-run.\n")
	return 1
end 'brokenRun'

// Record this run in the trend log.
//
// `--note` IS AN INSTRUCTION, NOT A REQUEST. If it was given, the row is written — full stop.
//
// This used to decline when allocations and bytes both matched the last logged row, on the theory
// that a row about nothing is noise. It is the wrong theory, and it fails SILENTLY, which is the
// worse half: the operator who typed `--note` asking for the datapoint got a line of prose and no
// row. A tool that second-guesses an explicit instruction from its operator, and then leaves the
// history it was told to write simply absent, is worse than one that logs a dull row — and the
// operator is the only party who knows why the run was made.
//
// The REPORT stays honest — an unmoved run is told it did not move — but the row is written.
//
// (Every guard that protects the log's INTEGRITY still refuses: a note carrying a `|` or a forged
// marker, a duplicate marker, a ladder of the wrong width, a curve set that is not this compiler's.
// Those reject BAD INPUT, which is a different thing entirely from overruling a valid one.)
function recordRun(trend LoggedMemory, results RungResultArray, note String) returns ExitCode
	if not anyMemoryMoved(trend, results: results) 'unchanged'
		print("\nscale-test: no change in allocations or bytes since the last logged run — recording anyway, because --note was given.\n")
	end 'unchanged'

	try recordScaleRun(results, note: note) otherwise (error) 'writeFailed'
		return brokenRun("the run could not be recorded in the trend log", detail: scaleErrorText(error))
	end 'writeFailed'
	return 0
end 'recordRun'

// Where the corpus is generated. `--emit-corpus=<dir>` puts it somewhere a human can read it;
// otherwise it goes to the gitignored `.scale-tmp/`, relative to the working directory — see
// CorpusRelativeRoot for why relative rather than absolute is load-bearing.
function corpusRootPath(args MaxonArgs) returns FilePath throws FilePathError
	if not args.emitCorpus.isEmpty() 'explicit'
		return try FilePath.from(args.emitCorpus)
	end 'explicit'
	return try FilePath.from(CorpusRelativeRoot)
end 'corpusRootPath'

// ===========================================================================================
// Compiling one rung
// ===========================================================================================

function compileRung(compilerExe FilePath, root FilePath, rung ScaleRung) returns RungResult throws ScaleError
	let metricsPath = try corpusRootFile(root, name: "{RungDirPrefix}{rung.index}{RungMetricsSuffix}")
	let outputBase = try corpusRootFile(root, name: "{RungDirPrefix}{rung.index}{RungOutputSuffix}")

	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(BuildSubcommandName)
	argv.push(rung.dir.toString())
	argv.push(OutputFlagName)
	argv.push(outputBase.toString())
	argv.push("--metrics={metricsPath.toString()}")

	let compile = try runProcess(compilerExe, argv: argv) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("compiler spawn failed: {e.displayReason()}")
	end 'spawnFailed'

	if compile.exitCode != 0 'compileFailed'
		throw ScaleError.reason("compiler exited {compile.exitCode}: {compile.stderr.trim()}")
	end 'compileFailed'

	// A MISSING metrics file is a failed rung, never a rung of zeroes. Zeroes would fit a curve —
	// a beautifully flat one — and report that the compiler had become infinitely fast.
	let text = try File.readText(metricsPath) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung.index} produced no metrics file at {metricsPath.toString()}")

	return try parseRungMetrics(text, rung: rung)
end 'compileRung'

// A file in the corpus root. `join` is overloaded on String and FilePath and a bare String constant
// matches both, so the component is bound to a FilePath first.
function corpusRootFile(root FilePath, name String) returns FilePath throws ScaleError
	let component = try FilePath.from(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("'{name}' is not a valid path component")
	return root.join(component)
end 'corpusRootFile'

// ===========================================================================================
// Parsing the metrics TSV (the format is MetricsEmit's; see its header)
// ===========================================================================================

function parseRungMetrics(text String, rung ScaleRung) returns RungResult throws ScaleError
	var phases = PhaseMetricArray.create()
	phases.resize(CompilePhase.allCases.count())
	var regAlloc = PhaseMetricArray.create()
	regAlloc.resize(RegAllocPhase.allCases.count())

	// `seen` is what turns a missing row into an error instead of a zero. A dense array seeded with
	// zeroes cannot otherwise tell "this phase did no work" from "this phase never reported".
	var phaseSeen = BoolArray.create()
	phaseSeen.resize(CompilePhase.allCases.count())

	var total = PhaseMetric.zero()
	var totalSeen = false
	var codeBytes = 0 as CodeByteCount
	var codeSeen = false

	for line in text.split("\n") 'eachLine'
		let trimmed = line.trim()
		if trimmed.isEmpty() or trimmed.startsWith("#") 'skip'
			continue
		end 'skip'

		let fields = trimmed.split("\t")
		if fields.count() != MetricsFieldCount 'malformed'
			throw ScaleError.reason("malformed metrics row (expected {MetricsFieldCount} tab-separated fields, got {fields.count()}): {trimmed}")
		end 'malformed'

		let kind = try fields.get(0) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: fields.get(0) OOB after count check")
		let name = try fields.get(1) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: fields.get(1) OOB after count check")
		let metric = try parseMetricFields(fields)

		if kind == MetricsKindTotal 'isTotal'
			total = metric
			totalSeen = true
		end 'isTotal' else if kind == MetricsKindPhase 'isPhase'
			let phase = try CompilePhase.fromName(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("metrics names an unknown phase '{name}'")
			try phases.set(phase.ordinal, value: metric) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: phases.set OOB for '{name}'")
			try phaseSeen.set(phase.ordinal, value: true) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: phaseSeen.set OOB for '{name}'")
		end 'isPhase' else if kind == MetricsKindRegAlloc 'isRegAlloc'
			let sub = try RegAllocPhase.fromName(name) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("metrics names an unknown regalloc sub-phase '{name}'")
			try regAlloc.set(sub.ordinal, value: metric) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: regAlloc.set OOB for '{name}'")
		end 'isRegAlloc' else if kind == MetricsKindCode 'isCode'
			codeBytes = metric.bytes as CodeByteCount
			codeSeen = true
		end 'isCode' else if kind == MetricsKindUnattributed 'isUnattributed'
			// Read and discarded: the compiler emits it so the two reconciliation panics run on
			// every metrics build (that is what makes them a tripwire), but the curves are fitted from
			// the phases themselves and from the total, both of which are already here.
			continue
		end 'isUnattributed' else 'unknownKind'
			throw ScaleError.reason("metrics row has an unknown kind '{kind}': {trimmed}")
		end 'unknownKind'
	end 'eachLine'

	if not totalSeen 'noTotal'
		throw ScaleError.reason("metrics for rung {rung.index} carried no `total` row")
	end 'noTotal'
	if not codeSeen 'noCode'
		throw ScaleError.reason("metrics for rung {rung.index} carried no `code` row — the corpus self-check cannot run without it")
	end 'noCode'

	for phase in CompilePhase.allCases 'checkSeen'
		let seen = try phaseSeen.get(phase.ordinal) otherwise panic("parseRungMetrics: phaseSeen.get OOB for '{phase.name}'")
		if not seen 'missing'
			throw ScaleError.reason("metrics for rung {rung.index} never reported phase '{phase.name}' — a phase added to CompilePhase but not to the metrics output would silently vanish from every curve and every trend column")
		end 'missing'
	end 'checkSeen'

	return RungResult.create(rung.index, sourceBytes: rung.sourceBytes, codeBytes: codeBytes, total: total, phases: phases, regAlloc: regAlloc)
end 'parseRungMetrics'

let MetricsFieldCount = 6

// Field 2 is the phase's NANOSECONDS, and it is skipped rather than read.
//
// The row is still required to HAVE six fields — the format is MetricsEmit's contract and a row with
// five is a malformed row, not a row with no clock — but the value is not carried anywhere. Reading
// it into a variable this suite then never used would be an invitation to put it back in a column.
let MetricsAllocsField = 3
let MetricsFreesField = 4
let MetricsBytesField = 5

function parseMetricFields(fields StringArray) returns PhaseMetric throws ScaleError
	let allocs = try parseCount(try fields.get(MetricsAllocsField) otherwise panic("parseMetricFields: allocs field OOB after count check"))
	let frees = try parseCount(try fields.get(MetricsFreesField) otherwise panic("parseMetricFields: frees field OOB after count check"))
	let bytes = try parseCount(try fields.get(MetricsBytesField) otherwise panic("parseMetricFields: bytes field OOB after count check"))
	return PhaseMetric.create(allocs, frees: frees, bytes: bytes)
end 'parseMetricFields'

export typealias ParsedCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// A non-negative decimal integer — THE one decimal parser in the suite (the metrics TSV, the trend
// log's grouped digits, `--rungs`, and the trace's `size=` fields all come through here). Every
// number any of them carries is a count, a byte volume, or a nanosecond reading, and none of them can
// be negative.
//
// The digit-count guard is not decoration: `--rungs=999999999999999999999` is user input, and
// without it the accumulator would run past `u64.max` and trap on the range rather than being
// rejected as the nonsense it is. Twenty digits is `u64.max`'s own width.
export function parseCount(raw String) returns ParsedCount throws ScaleError
	if raw.isEmpty() 'empty'
		throw ScaleError.reason("expected a number, got an empty field")
	end 'empty'

	let digits = raw.toByteArray()
	if digits.count() > MaxDecimalDigits 'tooManyDigits'
		throw ScaleError.reason("'{raw}' has {digits.count()} digits — no count in this suite exceeds {MaxDecimalDigits}")
	end 'tooManyDigits'

	var value = 0 as ParsedCount
	for digit in digits 'eachDigit'
		if digit < AsciiDigitZero or digit > AsciiDigitNine 'notADigit'
			throw ScaleError.reason("expected a number, got '{raw}'")
		end 'notADigit'
		value = value * 10 + (digit - AsciiDigitZero)
	end 'eachDigit'
	return value
end 'parseCount'

// The decimal width of u64.max — one more digit than any value the accumulator above can hold.
let MaxDecimalDigits = 20

let AsciiDigitZero = 0x30
let AsciiDigitNine = 0x39
// ===========================================================================================
// Reporting — data, and nothing but
// ===========================================================================================

let RungColumnWidth = 10 as ColumnWidth
let SizeColumnWidth = 12 as ColumnWidth
let CountColumnWidth = 14 as ColumnWidth
let GrowthColumnWidth = 9 as ColumnWidth

// The per-phase table's columns. `phase` is wide enough for `phase:elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
let PhaseColumnWidth = 28 as ColumnWidth
let PhaseCountColumnWidth = 12 as ColumnWidth

// THE LADDER'S OWN TABLE, and the `x` columns are what make it readable at a glance.
//
// Each rung DOUBLES the program, so a column of x2.00 is a compiler that is linear in its input and
// a column of x4.00 is one that is quadratic. Nothing is fitted and nothing is interpreted: the
// number printed is this rung's count divided by the one below it.
function printRungHeader()
	print("\n")
	print("{padRight("  rung", width: RungColumnWidth)}{padLeft("source", width: SizeColumnWidth)}{padLeft("code", width: SizeColumnWidth)}{padLeft("allocs", width: CountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("x", width: GrowthColumnWidth)}{padLeft("frees", width: CountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("bytes", width: CountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("x", width: GrowthColumnWidth)}\n")
end 'printRungHeader'

// `results` is the rungs climbed SO FAR, not including this one — the line is printed as each rung
// lands, so that a run whose top rung dies has still told you everything the rungs below it said.
function printRungLine(result RungResult, results RungResultArray)
	var line = padRight("  rung {result.rung}", width: RungColumnWidth)
	line.append(padLeft("{result.sourceBytes}", width: SizeColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.codeBytes}", width: SizeColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.total.allocs}", width: CountColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft(growthText(growthAgainstPrevious(result.total.allocs, results: results, ofBytes: false)), width: GrowthColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.total.frees}", width: CountColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft("{result.total.bytes}", width: CountColumnWidth))
	line.append(padLeft(growthText(growthAgainstPrevious(result.total.bytes, results: results, ofBytes: true)), width: GrowthColumnWidth))
	print("{line}\n")
end 'printRungLine'

// This rung's growth over the LAST rung in `results` — which is the rung below it, because the
// caller has not pushed this one yet. Rung 0 has nothing below it and says so.
function growthAgainstPrevious(now MemoryCount, results RungResultArray, ofBytes bool) returns GrowthReading
	if results.count() == 0 'bottomRung'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'bottomRung'

	let below = try results.get(results.count() - 1) otherwise panic("growthAgainstPrevious: count > 0 but the last result is missing")
	return growthOf(now, previous: measureOf(below.total, ofBytes: ofBytes))
end 'growthAgainstPrevious'

// WHICH of a phase's two published measures a table is about. One `bool` threaded through one
// accessor, rather than two copies of every table function differing only in the field they read —
// the allocations table and the bytes table are the same table asked a different question.
//
// FREES ARE NOT A THIRD TABLE. They track allocations almost exactly (a compile that allocates N and
// frees N-k has k live at exit, and k is small and constant), so a third copy of this table would be
// a near-duplicate paid for in a screenful. They are measured, printed per rung above, and carried
// per phase in `--result-json` for anyone who wants them.
export function measureOf(metric PhaseMetric, ofBytes bool) returns MemoryCount
	if ofBytes 'wantBytes'
		return metric.bytes
	end 'wantBytes'

	return metric.allocs
end 'measureOf'

// EVERY PHASE, AT EVERY RUNG — the table that localizes a movement.
//
// The rung table above says the compiler's allocations moved. This one says WHICH PHASE'S did, and
// the `x` column says whether that phase is growing linearly with the program or faster than it. That
// is the whole diagnostic loop: the total moved (rung table) → this phase moved (this table) → this
// TYPE, from this FUNCTION (`--per-type`).
//
// The regalloc sub-phases are printed under the phases, not among them: they are a BREAKDOWN of
// `phase:regalloc` and adding them to the same list would double-count every allocation they make.
function printPhaseTable(results RungResultArray, ofBytes bool)
	print("\n")
	print("  per-phase {measureHeading(ofBytes)}, and its growth over the rung below (the ladder DOUBLES: x2.00 is linear)\n")
	print("\n")

	var header = padRight("  phase", width: PhaseColumnWidth)
	for result in results 'eachRung'
		header.append(padLeft("rung {result.rung}", width: PhaseCountColumnWidth))
	end 'eachRung'
	header.append(padLeft("x", width: GrowthColumnWidth))
	print("{header}\n")

	for source in scalePhaseSources() 'eachPhase'
		printPhaseRow(source, results: results, ofBytes: ofBytes)
	end 'eachPhase'
end 'printPhaseTable'

function measureHeading(ofBytes bool) returns String
	if ofBytes 'wantBytes'
		return "BYTES"
	end 'wantBytes'

	return "ALLOCATIONS"
end 'measureHeading'

function printPhaseRow(source PhaseSource, results RungResultArray, ofBytes bool)
	var line = padRight("  {phaseSourceName(source)}", width: PhaseColumnWidth)

	for result in results 'eachRung'
		line.append(padLeft("{measureOf(phaseMetricOf(source, result: result), ofBytes: ofBytes)}", width: PhaseCountColumnWidth))
	end 'eachRung'

	line.append(padLeft(growthText(topRungGrowth(source, results: results, ofBytes: ofBytes)), width: GrowthColumnWidth))
	print("{line}\n")
end 'printPhaseRow'

// The growth of the TOP rung over the one below it — the steepest, most informative end of the
// ladder, and the one a phase that has gone superlinear shows it in first.
function topRungGrowth(source PhaseSource, results RungResultArray, ofBytes bool) returns GrowthReading
	if results.count() < MinRungsForGrowth 'tooFewRungs'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'tooFewRungs'

	let top = try results.get(results.count() - 1) otherwise panic("topRungGrowth: the ladder is not empty")
	let below = try results.get(results.count() - 2) otherwise panic("topRungGrowth: the ladder has at least two rungs")

	return growthOf(measureOf(phaseMetricOf(source, result: top), ofBytes: ofBytes), previous: measureOf(phaseMetricOf(source, result: below), ofBytes: ofBytes))
end 'topRungGrowth'

// What the compiler allocated, against what it allocated when the trend log was last written.
//
// THIS IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE INSTRUMENT. These numbers are bit-for-bit reproducible, so every
// difference here is REAL — the same input through the same compiler cannot produce a different one
// by accident. It is worth explaining rather than merely noticing.
function printMemoryTrend(results RungResultArray, trend LoggedMemory)
	print("\n")
	match trend 'm'
		absent then printNoPreviousRun()
		present(date, note, allocs, bytes) then printMemoryDelta(results, date: date, note: note, loggedAllocs: allocs, loggedBytes: bytes)
	end 'm'
end 'printMemoryTrend'

function printNoPreviousRun()
	print("  {OptimizationLogRelativePath} has no rows yet — this run is the first datapoint, so there is\n")
	print("  nothing to difference it against. Record it with --note=\"<what changed and why>\".\n")
end 'printNoPreviousRun'

let TrendRungColumnWidth = 8 as ColumnWidth
let TrendCountColumnWidth = 16 as ColumnWidth
let TrendDeltaColumnWidth = 14 as ColumnWidth

function printMemoryDelta(results RungResultArray, date String, note String, loggedAllocs AllocCountArray, loggedBytes ByteCountArray)
	print("  vs the last row of {OptimizationLogRelativePath} — {date}, \"{note}\"\n")
	print("\n")
	print("{padRight("  rung", width: TrendRungColumnWidth)}{padLeft("allocs", width: TrendCountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("delta", width: TrendDeltaColumnWidth)}{padLeft("bytes", width: TrendCountColumnWidth)}{padLeft("delta", width: TrendDeltaColumnWidth)}\n")

	var moved = false
	for i in 0 upto results.count() 'eachRung'
		let result = try results.get(i) otherwise panic("printMemoryDelta: i < count but results.get({i}) failed")

		var line = padRight("  {result.rung}", width: TrendRungColumnWidth)
		line.append(padLeft(groupDigits(result.total.allocs as Delta), width: TrendCountColumnWidth))
		line.append(padLeft(countDeltaText(result.total.allocs, logged: loggedAllocs, rung: i), width: TrendDeltaColumnWidth))
		line.append(padLeft(groupDigits(result.total.bytes as Delta), width: TrendCountColumnWidth))
		line.append(padLeft(countDeltaText(result.total.bytes, logged: loggedBytes, rung: i), width: TrendDeltaColumnWidth))
		print("{line}\n")

		if countMoved(result.total.allocs, logged: loggedAllocs, rung: i) or countMoved(result.total.bytes, logged: loggedBytes, rung: i) 'changed'
			moved = true
		end 'changed'
	end 'eachRung'

	if not moved 'unchanged'
		print("\n  Every rung matches the last logged run exactly. The compiler's memory traffic is unchanged.\n")
		return
	end 'unchanged'

	print("\n  A number here MOVED, and it moved for a reason: these counts are bit-for-bit reproducible,\n")
	print("  so the same input through the same compiler cannot produce a different one by accident.\n")
	print("  Explain it, and record it with --note — the instrument can see WHAT moved and never why.\n")
end 'printMemoryDelta'

// A rung this run climbed that the logged row did not have is not a delta of zero; it is not a delta
// at all. (`assertRecordable` refuses to LOG such a run, but a --rungs=3 run with no --note is a
// perfectly reasonable thing to do while iterating, and it still prints.)
function countDeltaText(current AllocCount, logged AllocCountArray, rung LadderIndex) returns String
	let before = try logged.get(rung) otherwise 'notLogged'
		return NoBaselineText
	end 'notLogged'
	return signedDeltaText(current as Delta, before: before)
end 'countDeltaText'

function countMoved(current AllocCount, logged AllocCountArray, rung LadderIndex) returns bool
	let before = try logged.get(rung) otherwise 'notLogged'
		return true
	end 'notLogged'
	return current != before
end 'countMoved'

function signedDeltaText(current Delta, before Delta) returns String
	if current > before 'grew'
		return "+{groupDigits(current - before)}"
	end 'grew'
	return groupDigits(current - before)
end 'signedDeltaText'

// A position in the ladder — an index into the parallel per-rung arrays (this run's results, and
// the logged run's columns), which is NOT the same thing as `RungIndex`: those arrays may be shorter
// than the ladder the log recorded, and that is exactly the case the delta report has to handle.
export typealias LadderIndex = int(0 to u64.max)

// The corpus self-check's own numbers. They are PRINTED, not merely consulted: a reader looking at a
// set of tidy proportional memory numbers is entitled to see the evidence that they are numbers about
// a program that was actually compiled, rather than one that folded away to nothing.
function printCorpusGrowth(results RungResultArray)
	print("\n")
	if results.count() < MinRungsForGrowth 'tooFewRungs'
		print("  corpus: {results.count()} rung(s) — too few to say anything about GROWTH, this check included.\n")
		return
	end 'tooFewRungs'

	var line = "  corpus: emitted code grows"
	for i in 1 upto results.count() 'eachRung'
		line.append(" {growthText(codeGrowthAt(results, rung: i))}")
	end 'eachRung'

	print("{line} per rung (floor {growthText(GrowthReading.measured(MinCodeGrowthRatio))}) —\n")
	print("          the programs compiled rather than folding away, so the numbers above are numbers\n")
	print("          about something.\n")
end 'printCorpusGrowth'
